{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אשר ישחט מחוץ למחנה", :translit "asher yishachat michutz lamachaneh", :gloss "which one slaughters outside the camp"} {:he "פרשת שחוטי חוץ", :translit "parashat shchutei chutz", :gloss "the pericope of slaughterers outside"} {:he "שעיר המשתלח", :translit "se'ir hamishtalei'ach", :gloss "the sent-goat"} {:he "צוק", :translit "tzuk", :gloss "cliff"} {:he "סלעים שיש להם כמין שינים בולטין", :translit "sela'im sheyesh lahem kemin shinayim boltin", :gloss "rocks that have like protruding teeth"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parashot: juxtaposition of the pericope of outside-slaughter with the scapegoat pericope to draw a legal correspondence."}], :frame {:text "The scapegoat is sent to Azazel in the wilderness on Yom Kippur (Leviticus 16:21–22). The Mishnah records that the goat was pushed backward off a cliff: 'They pushed him backward, and he would be dismembered limb by limb' (Yoma 6:6). The Sifra derives the prohibition of slaughtering consecrated animals outside the Sanctuary from Leviticus 17:3–4 (Sifra, Shemini, parashah 2). The Ba'al HaTurim reads the sequence of the biblical text as itself conveying the contrast: Scripture just treated the scapegoat, whose destruction outside the camp is legitimate, and now legislates that all other offerings slaughtered outside are prohibited.", :sources ["Leviticus 16:21-22" "Yoma 6:6" "Sifra Shemini parashah 2" "Leviticus 17:3-4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parashot: juxtaposition of the pericope of outside-slaughter with the scapegoat pericope to draw a legal correspondence.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:23:52.415790581Z", :model "glm-5.2"}